"summary":"Simplest: pass the parameters in registers  In some cases, may be more parameters than registers Parameters stored in a block, or table, in memory, and address of block passed as a parameter in a register  This approach taken by Linux and Solaris","size":6},{"label":"starts, bootstrap, blocks","concepts":[117,96,123,56,75,77],"chunks":[70],"summary":"Small piece of code – bootstrap loader, BIOS, stored in ROM or EEPROM locates the kernel, loads it into memory, and starts it Sometimes two-step process where boot block at fixed location loaded by ROM code, which loads bootstrap loader from disk","size":6},{"label":"completion, program counter, handling","concepts":[124,81,148,49,21],"chunks":[8,17,9,18],"summary":"After I/O starts, control returns to user program only upon I/O completion Providing mechanisms for deadlock handling The operating system is responsible for the following activities in connection with process management:","size":5},{"label":"implementation, goals, user goals","concepts":[113,59,2,16],"chunks":[60,2,62],"summary":"Design and Implementation of OS is not “solvable”, but some approaches have proven successful User goals – operating system should be convenient to use, easy to learn, reliable, safe, and fast","size":4},{"label":"computer storage, networking measurements, bit","concepts":[0,37,128,87,99],"chunks":[11,26],"summary":"Storage Definitions and Notation Review The basic unit of computer storage is the bit. Networking measurements are an exception to this general rule; they are given in bits (because networks move data a bit at a time).","size":5},{"label":"dynamic random-access memory, hard disk drives, non-volatile memory","concepts":[7,11,38,72,140,149],"chunks":[10],"summary":"Typically random-access memory in the form of Dynamic Random-access Memory (DRAM) Non-volatile memory (NVM) devices– faster than hard disks, nonvolatile","size":6},{"label":"data structures, kernel data, bitmap","concepts":[25,28,69],"chunks":[39,0],"summary":"Many similar to standard programming data structures Balanced binary search tree is O(lg n) Kernel Data Structures","size":3}],"built_s":0.139}